editorialMd:
Build byDistance

Sort `readings` by distance from 0, from nearest to farthest. This distance is called absolute value: both -4 and 4 have distance 4. When two numbers have the same distance, put the smaller number first. Keep all repeated numbers. For [3, -2, 2, -3], `byDistance` is [-2, 2, -3, 3].

Return the answer

Count the numbers in `byDistance` that are at least `k` (greater than or equal to `k`). Store the count in `atLeastK`. Complete `magnitudeOrderThresholdFile` so it returns `atLeastK`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `byDistance` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n log n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function magnitudeOrderThresholdFile(readings, k) {
  const a=readings; let out=[];
  out=[...a].sort((x,y)=>Math.abs(x)-Math.abs(y)||x-y);
  return out.filter(x=>x>=k).length;
}


